    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that \"betting\" analogy makes a little more sense but i'm still hung up on the \"invisible\" part. you said it's like making a bet on the frequency, but where does that frequency actually come from in the signal? is there some kind of underlying pattern that just exists in the data even if the signal looks totally random or noisy? how does the math know which frequencies are \"real\" versus just noise?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Even in seemingly random or noisy signals, the Fourier Transform is capable of unveiling underlying patterns. The math doesn't literally \"bet\" on frequencies, but rather, it exploits the symmetry of the sine waves and the orthogonality of complex exponentials. In simpler words, the Fourier Transform is picking out the components of the signal that vary predictably and slowly, usually periodic or harmonic, while filtering out random or high-frequency variations. This is why even in noisy or random data, the transform can often reveal meaning behind the noise.",
      "source": "target",
